'''''Angiopteris evecta''''', commonly known as the '''king fern''', '''giant fern''', '''elephant fern''', '''oriental vessel fern''', '''Madagascar tree fern''', or '''mule's foot fern''', is a very large rainforest fern in the family [[Marattiaceae]] native to most parts of [[Southeast Asia]] and [[Oceania]]. It has a history dating back about 300 million years, and is believed to have the longest fronds of any fern in the world.

==Description==
''Angiopteris evecta'' is a self-supporting evergreen [[perennial]] fern with very large [[bipinnate]] fronds. The trunk-like [[rhizome]] is massive, measuring up to {{cvt|1|m}} in diameter. The older portions of the rhizome lie on the ground while the newer growth may rise vertically up to {{cvt|1.2|m}} high.

The arching, glossy green fronds, which emerge from the tip of the rhizome, may reach up to {{cvt|9|m}} long and {{cvt|2.5|m}} wide, with the fleshy green [[Petiole (botany)|petiole]] (leaf stem) making up {{cvt|2|m}} of that length.{{R|ABG|CABI|CIB|GISD}} They are said to be the longest fern fronds in the world,{{R|QNPGF|ABCL|WTMA}} and despite their enormous size they have no woody strengthening tissues in the fronds to keep them erect—instead they are supported entirely by the [[turgor pressure |hydraulic pressure]] of the sap.{{R|JONES}} On either side of the petiole where it arises from the rhizome there are flat, rounded, leathery, ear-shaped [[stipule]]s, known as "auricles",{{R|ABCG|ANSPA}} which can measure up to {{cvt|15|cm}} in diameter.

The fronds are [[Pinnation#Iteration of divisions |bipinnate]] with about 9 to 12 pairs of pinnae measuring up to {{cvt|150|cm}} long and {{cvt|45|cm}} wide. Each pinnae carries about 30 to 40 pairs of pinnules that measure around {{convert|13|by|2|cm|1|abbr=on}}, and both the main rachis and the secondary rachillae (midribs) are [[pulvinus|pulvinate]] (swollen at the base). This pulvinus is responsible for raising and lowering the frond, and is up to {{cvt|20|cm}} diameter.<ref>{{cite book | last= Setchell | first= William A. | date= June 1924 |title= American Samoa - Carnegie Institute publication Number 341 | location= Washington, D.C. | publisher= Carnegie Institute | page= 121 }}</ref>| {{R|CABI|FOA}} Sporangia are borne on the underside of the pinnules, very close to the margin, in clusters of 5 to 8 opposite pairs.{{R|CIB|JONES}} Overall dimensions of this fern can be up to {{convert|7|m|ft|abbr=on}} high by {{convert|16|m|ft|abbr=on}} wide.{{R|ABG|CIB|GISD|FOC|NSWE}}

===Evolution===
Fossilised fronds bearing a distinct similarity to this plant have been found in [[Paleozoic]] rocks from every continent,{{R|JONES}} a time when ferns and their relatives were the dominant plants on the planet.{{R|QNPGF|ABCL|WTMA|JONES}} The geographically isolated communities seen today point to favourable climatic conditions being more widespread in the past.{{R|ABG}}

==Taxonomy==
[[File:021 Angiopteris evecta ryubintai.jpg|thumb|160px]]
''Angiopteris evecta'' was originally named and described as ''Polypodium evectum'' by [[Georg Forster]] in 1786, in his book ''[[Florulae Insularum Australium Prodromus]]''.{{R|APNI1}} It was moved to the genus ''Angiopteris'' in 1794 by [[Georg Franz Hoffmann]], publishing in the journal ''[[Commentationes Societatis Regiae Scientiarum Gottingensis]]''.{{R|APNI2|HATHITRUST}} ''A. evecta'' is the [[type species]] of the genus ''[[Angiopteris]]''.{{R|IPNI2}}

===Etymology===
The genus name comes from Ancient Greek [[wikt:ἀγγεῖον|''aggeion'']] 'vessel', and [[wikt:Pteris|''pteris'']], 'fern', and is a reference to the sporangia.{{R|FOA}} The [[Botanical name#Binary name|species epithet]] is the Latin adjective ''evectus'' meaning to carry out, bring forth, raise, or elevate.{{R|LEWIS}}

